+### "`arend -t` says `Failed: 2` but prints no errors"
+
+The test runner only counts failures. Typecheck the test file as a throwaway library to see the messages: copy it to `/tmp/probe/src/Probe.ard`, write `/tmp/probe/arend.yaml` with `dependencies: [arend-lib]`, and run `arend --no-daemon -L <dir containing arend-lib> Probe` from `/tmp/probe`.
+
+### "A module's goals are missing from a multi-module check"
+
+In `arend -r -c A B C`, a module that is first typechecked *as a dependency* of an earlier target does not get its `[GOAL]` lines printed, although the summary still counts it (`◯`, "Number of modules with goals"). When auditing goals, list targets in dependency order or check modules one at a time.
 
+### Daemon hygiene
+
+A daemon fixes `-r`, `-c`, `--serialize`, `-L` at start-up and ignores them per request — a fresh verification run needs `--no-daemon`. After renaming or deleting a module, restart it (`arend --daemon-stop`, then `arend -d --serialize`) and delete the stale `.arc`; after ordinary edits `arend --daemon-refresh` suffices. `-fu`/`-ss --json` write their `[INFO]` line to stderr — parse stdout only. `-fu --json` reports the *enclosing declaration* of each usage, which is enough to compute which declarations transitively depend on a given one (e.g. on an intentional `{?}`).
+
+### Pruning imports
+
+Unused imports are found reliably by trial: remove one `\import` line, typecheck the module through the daemon, keep the removal only if there are no errors and the goal count is unchanged. Arend imports are not transitive, so removals cannot break other modules.
